{"key": "multifieldqa_en#5", "dataset": "multifieldqa_en", "idx": 5, "prompt_tokens": 11554, "inductor_fused": true, "answers": ["It tends to suppress the Kondo effect."], "all_classes": null, "length": 5009, "method": "dense", "params": {}, "prefill_s": 5.1585083000099985, "compress_s": 2.3600005079060793e-05, "decode_s": 9.828041300002951, "decode_tps": 6.511979146850022, "ttft_s": 5.1585319000150776, "peak_prefill_gb": 8.259557376, "peak_decode_gb": 8.361447424, "kv_physical_gb": 1.89300736, "kv_dense_equiv_gb": 1.89300736, "kv_compression_x": 1.0, "kv_footprint_realized": true, "attn_eager": false, "reads_attention_weights": false, "gen_tokens": 64, "text": "The proximity of superconductivity induces non-local pairing (via CAR exchange) that can screen the Kondo effect, leading to a two-stage Kondo screening even in the absence of other exchange mechanisms. This screening is characterized by a residual low-temperature conductance and depends on the superconducting coupling strength."}
